; rfc-9597 — the CWT Claims header parameter, as a usable grammar
; DERIVED - not verbatim. The source's own four lines of CDDL are in
; schema/source-renderings.txt, and THE SOURCE DECLARES THEM NON-NORMATIVE in
; the sentence immediately before them: "The following is a non-normative
; description for the value type of the CWT claim header parameter using CDDL
; [RFC8610]." (§2). So that fragment constrains nothing, and this file is the
; library's rendering of what §1 and §2's NORMATIVE sentences actually fix.
; Cite RFC 9597's prose, never this file.
;
; WHAT THE SOURCE'S OWN CDDL SAYS, FOR COMPARISON (verbatim, §2):
;     CWT-Claims = {
;      * Claim-Label => any
;     }
;     Claim-Label = int / text
; That admits the EMPTY MAP, admits any label type the `int / text` union
; covers, and forbids nothing. The rules below add only what a normative
; sentence of RFC 9597 states, and each carries its locator.

; The COSE header buckets are RFC 9052 §3's, named here and not redefined.
; RFC 9597 adds ONE label to them.
cwt-claims-label = 15                 ; §2 Table 1

; §2 Table 1: Value Type `map`. §2's CDDL names the value type CWT-Claims.
CWT-Claims = {* Claim-Label => any}   ; §2, NON-NORMATIVE in the source
Claim-Label = int / text              ; §2, NON-NORMATIVE in the source

; THE PLACEMENT RULES, WHICH ARE THE NORMATIVE PART AND ARE NOT IN THE SOURCE'S
; CDDL AT ALL.
;
;   §2: "It is RECOMMENDED that the CWT Claims header parameter only be used in
;        a protected header to avoid the contents being malleable."
;   §2: "The header parameter MUST only occur once in either the protected or
;        unprotected header of a COSE structure."
;
; The MUST bars label 15 from appearing in BOTH buckets. It PERMITS it in the
; unprotected one. RFC 9052 §4.4's Sig_structure is
; [context, body_protected, external_aad, payload], so a conformant producer can
; emit CWT claims that no signature covers. CDDL cannot express "at most once
; across two maps", so the three shapes are enumerated and the socket a profile
; must close is named.
;
; WHICH READING THIS FILE ENCODES — READING 1 of ../design-notes.md D-6: "at
; most once ACROSS the two buckets". That is the only reading under which the
; enumeration below is COMPLETE. Under D-6's reading 2 ("at most once PER
; bucket") a fourth shape is conformant — label 15 in both buckets, once each —
; and the enumeration is incomplete rather than wrong. The document
; disambiguates none of the three readings; D-6 argues reading 1 and this file
; follows it, so a profile enforcing "not in both" is enforcing OUR reading of
; an ambiguous MUST. Stated here 2026-10-21 by pass 3 (cross-check): pass 2
; found D-6 but recorded it only in ../design-notes.md, ../README.md and
; ../../record.yaml, and this file — the one the ambiguity is about — did not
; say which reading it took. ../requirements.yaml R-0021 and ../messages.yaml
; carry the same statement.

cose-header-with-cwt-claims =
      cwt-claims-protected-only       ; the §2 RECOMMENDED shape
    / cwt-claims-unprotected-only     ; CONFORMANT and unsigned - see above
    / cwt-claims-absent               ; label 15 is optional; §2 adds no MUST

cwt-claims-protected-only = [
  protected: bstr .cbor {cwt-claims-label => CWT-Claims, * label => any},
  unprotected: {* nonclaims-label => any}
]

cwt-claims-unprotected-only = [
  protected: bstr .cbor {* nonclaims-label => any},
  unprotected: {cwt-claims-label => CWT-Claims, * nonclaims-label => any}
]

cwt-claims-absent = [
  protected: bstr .cbor {* nonclaims-label => any},
  unprotected: {* nonclaims-label => any}
]

label = int / tstr                    ; RFC 9052 §1.5
nonclaims-label = label .ne 15        ; this library's rule, not the source's

; [correction 2026-10-20, pass 2 - verify] `label` was cited as RFC 9052 §1.4.
; It is §1.5. §1.4 is "CDDL Grammar for CBOR Data Structures", which defines
; `start` and `Internal_Types`; `label = int / tstr` and `values = any` are in
; §1.5, "CBOR-Related Terminology". Re-derived from records/ietf/rfc-9052's own
; cose.cddl and normative.md rather than from this file. The production itself
; was correct. rfc-9596's typ-header.derived.cddl carried the same error.
;
; [pass 2] `nonclaims-label = label .ne 15` CHECKED against RFC 8610 §3.8 and
; §3.8.6 directly, not against this file. §3.8's syntax is "target
; .control-operator controller", so `label .ne 15` is well formed with `15` as
; a controller "containing just that single value". §3.8.6 states ".ne and .eq
; are defined for both numeric values and values of other types" and that "All
; other cases are not equal (e.g., comparing a text string with a byte
; string)" - so a `tstr` target satisfies `.ne 15` rather than erroring, and
; this production admits every text label and every integer but 15, which is
; the intent. The construct is correct.

; WHAT CANNOT BE EXPRESSED HERE, AND IS THEREFORE A PROFILE'S JOB:
;
;   1. §2's duplication MUST - "In cases where CWT claims are present both in
;      the payload and the header of a CWT, an application receiving such a
;      structure MUST verify that their values are identical, unless the
;      application defines other specific processing rules for these claims."
;      A cross-structure equality predicate is outside CDDL, AND the rule is
;      scoped to "a CWT", which rfc-8392 §6 declines to define: "How to
;      determine that a CBOR data structure is a CWT is application dependent."
;      AND "identical" is undefined - byte equality of the encoded items, or
;      equality of the CBOR data items? A preferred and a non-preferred encoding
;      of the same integer are one value and two byte strings.
;
;   2. §1's MUST NOT - "Recipients of the CWT Claims header parameter MUST NOT
;      use the information in the CWT Claims header parameter beyond the
;      integrity protection or authentication afforded to the CWT Claims header
;      and the information used to derive its intended interpretation." That is
;      a constraint on USE, not on bytes.
;
;   3. Which claims must be present. RFC 9597 requires none. rfc-9943 §6 is the
;      only ratified profile the library holds that does: label 15 in the
;      protected header with iss (1) and sub (2).
